Six new repositories alongside the thirteen existing ones, all tenant-bound per the module contract at :1-7 and all using prepared statements like the rest: approvals {get, create, upsert, decide, partition, listByTenant}; trustLedger {get, list, upsert}; capabilities {get, append, seen(tenantId, nonce)}; actionRecords {findByNonce, append, listByTenant, removeSeededNonces(tenantId, nonces)}; killSwitches {get, isActive(scope, scopeId), set, clear, listActive}; guardianIncidents {append, newest}.

THE IDEMPOTENCY REPOSITORY GAINS ONE METHOD, AND IT IS NAMED HERE BECAUSE THE RESET PREVIOUSLY CALLED A METHOD THAT DOES NOT EXIST. createIdempotencyRepository currently returns a flat {claim, record, release, get} over idempotency_keys, whose key column is event_id, not nonce, and there is no keys sub-namespace. The reset therefore calls repositories.idempotency.removeByNonces(TENANT.id, nonces, 'executor') with NO .keys, and this change adds that method to the EXISTING repository, deleting WHERE tenant_id = ? AND event_id IN (?, ?, ...) AND consumer = ?. It has to be a real method: if the reset skipped it, a claim row from the previous run would survive, the executor's step 1b claim for apr_seed_budget_1 would lose to it, and the second approval would return {executed:false, duplicate:true, receipt_id} against a receipt that step 2 already deleted, so the second approval never executes. That is precisely what qa_script step 5 and test/data/seed.test.js's 'the second receipt id DIFFERS' case exist to catch.

THE SINGLE PENDING-VERSUS-LAPSED PREDICATE, WHICH IS THE WHOLE OF THE SECOND OBJECTION. This module exports one pure function and every consumer of approval state in the repository goes through it:

  export function isPendingApproval(row, nowIso) {
    return row.status === 'pending' && Date.parse(row.expires_at) > Date.parse(nowIso);
  }

and one method built on it: approvals.partition(tenantId, nowIso) -> { pending, executed, lapsed }, where pending is every row where isPendingApproval is true, executed is status 'executed', and lapsed is everything else that is a terminal or expired decision: status 'lapsed' or 'rejected', or status 'pending' whose expires_at has passed. The comparison is STRICTLY greater, so an approval whose expiry is exactly the evaluation instant is lapsed, and that boundary is pinned by a test rather than left to a reader. Nothing in the repository re-implements the comparison. There are exactly four consumers and each names this function rather than writing expires_at arithmetic: (1) GET /v1/approvals, whose three buckets are the partition() object verbatim; (2) the approvals page, whose queue is partition().pending and whose lapsed and executed sections are partition().lapsed and partition().executed; (3) the decide route's step-5 non-pending check, which refuses with APPROVAL_NOT_PENDING on exactly the rows the page would render as lapsed; (4) the test and qa_script assertions that count pending rows, which count partition().pending.length and nothing else. A null or unparseable expires_at is NOT pending: an approval whose expiry cannot be read has not been shown to be live, and admitting it is the fail-open TR-24 forbids.

removeSeededNonces(tenantId, nonces) takes a LIST and binds it as nonce IN (?, ?, ...). It is the reset's only deletion primitive and it takes no prefix argument: the caller hands it a list of nonces, and the list is built by calling decisionNonce. There is no method in this module whose behaviour is described by a string prefix, because a prefix is a convention duplicated into a second place and the last two rejections were about exactly that duplication.
